window.docContents[165].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.CliElf/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___CliElf":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession › CliElf.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 6 headline theorems depend on it 74 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. A statement file: its definitions say what the headline theorems claim (games, adversaries, bounds). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.This file is in the non-default library CliElfLib (its theorems need the ELF built from source).\n\n\n\nThe libsignal-cli ELF, built from sourceTrusted: the statement. cliElfBytes is the raw byte sequence of .GeneratedLean/cli-elf/libsignal-cli.elf, read at elaboration time via file_bytes% (Arm64/Trusted/Process.lean), which verifies the SHA-256 digest pinned below.Where the file comes from. It is not committed (.GeneratedLean/ is gitignored). scripts/cli-elf-stock --stdbin --build builds it from source with stock LLVM ld.lld (-static -no-pie --no-relax --gc-sections, linker script third_party/libsignal/libsignal-session/cli-elf/stock/stock_c2.ld, base 0x400000), with the libsignal revision, rustc 1.98.1 (and its aarch64-unknown-linux-musl self-contained/ CRT objects and libc.a), zig (for rdl_shim.S), ld.lld version prefix, std-main entry crate, and arm64-semantics commit pinned in third_party/libsignal/libsignal-session/cli-elf/stock/inputs_stock_stdbin.lock, from the same Rust objects that rust-obj2lean reads: the compiled crate objects and trimmed alloc/std sysroot members of libsignal-session/translation.toml (cliLinkObjs, extracted into libsignalSessionLib = libsignalSessionMultiLib), and the libsignal-cli-io crate object of cli/io/translation.toml (SignalSessionIoPlacedObj). In CI, the parallel cli-elf-build job builds the ELF from source, checks its SHA-256 and link metadata against the lock and this module's pin (scripts/cli-elf-stock --stdbin --verify-pin --lean-mod LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliElf.lean), checks structural invariants and separate-path-depth deterministic reproducibility (scripts/cli-elf-stock --stdbin --verify and --check), and runs the A-LINK linker-resolution check (scripts/stock-pin/alink_check.py --lock third_party/libsignal/libsignal-session/cli-elf/stock/m4_stock.lock); the lake build job then downloads the verified ELF artifact, re-checks --verify-pin, and builds and audits the lake library CliElfLib (which owns this module and lists .GeneratedLean/cli-elf/libsignal-cli.elf as an input_file; no default lake root imports this module). These jobs and steps run unconditionally on every push to main (and workflow_dispatch) and on pull_requests touching the ELF's byte or build inputs, trusted specifications, or the CliElfLib-only proof modules; a PR that modifies only shared upstream proof modules skips them, so any breakage it introduces in the CliElfLib-only modules surfaces on the first main push that contains it, and the binary-level verification claim refers to a main commit SHA whose cli-elf-build and lake build jobs ran the CLI ELF steps green.What the pin means. Anyone can rebuild the ELF from source and compare its SHA-256 with the pin. The pin is the only place where a byte of the ELF is named. Every address the statement uses comes from the ELF itself (ElfImage.parse, loadState, and .symtab lookup via resolveCliElfAddrs in CliBinaryPq.lean).Main definitions* cliElfBytes: the raw bytes of the pinned libsignal-cli ELF, the subject of every binary-level CLI statement (CliBinaryPq.lean, StatementPqElf.lean, ElfBinding.lean, Binary.lean).\n\n\n\ndef cliElfBytesL66–72 ↗#Raw bytes of the static ARM64 libsignal-cli ELF (ET_EXEC, musl), verified against its pinned digest by file_bytes%.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliElf.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.CliElf/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___CliElf"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.DH.X25519/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___DH___X25519":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/DH › X25519.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 8 headline theorems depend on it 65 of 113 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (11)abbrev a24def decodeU255structure MontProjPointdef montDiffAddAndDoubledef montLadderLoopdef x25519Ladderdef x25519def x25519Basepointdef x25519Pubkeydef x25519Refdef x25519PubFromSecretRef\n\nRFC 7748 X25519 SpecificationTrusted: definitions used by the statement. Direct transcription of RFC 7748 (§5, §6.1) for Curve25519 Montgomery-ladder Diffie-Hellman (x25519Ref : CryptoGames.Ratchet.X25519Fn) and basepoint public-key derivation (x25519PubFromSecretRef : CryptoGames.LibsignalSession.PubKeyFn). See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.\n\n\n\nabbrev a24L37–38 ↗#Montgomery curve constant $a_{24} = (486662 - 2) / 4 = 121665$ (RFC 7748 §5).\n\ndef decodeU255L40–43 ↗#Decode a 32-byte little-endian Montgomery $u$-coordinate by masking bit 255 (RFC 7748 §5 decodeUCoordinate(u, 255)).\n\nstructure MontProjPointL45–50 ↗#Projective Kummer-line point $(U : W)$ on Montgomery Curve25519 $B v^2 = u^3 + 486662 u^2 + u$ representing $u = U \\cdot W^{-1} \\bmod p$ (with $(1 : 0)$ denoting $\\mathcal{O}$).\n\ndef montDiffAddAndDoubleL52–70 ↗#One combined differential-addition and point-doubling step on the Curve25519 Kummer line (RFC 7748 §5 / Costello-Smith 2017 Algorithm 8): given $u_1 = u(P - Q)$ and $(P, Q)$, returns $([2]P, P + Q)$.\n\ndef montLadderLoopL72–81 ↗#255-bit big-endian Montgomery ladder loop (RFC 7748 §5 / MontgomeryPoint::mul_bits_be): processes bits fuel - 1 down to 0 of scalar k.\n\ndef x25519LadderL83–88 ↗#Pure integer-to-field RFC 7748 Montgomery ladder: reduces u mod $p$, runs 255 steps of montLadderLoop with scalar k, and returns $U \\cdot W^{p - 2} \\bmod p$.\n\ndef x25519L90–95 ↗#RFC 7748 §5 X25519(k, u) Diffie-Hellman function (StaticSecret::from(k).diffie_hellman(&PublicKey::from(u))): clamps scalar, decodes uBytes mod $2^{255}$ and mod $p$, runs the 255-bit Montgomery ladder, and encodes $U \\cdot W^{p - 2} \\bmod p$ as 32 little-endian bytes.\n\ndef x25519BasepointL97–99 ↗#Standard Curve25519 Montgomery basepoint $u = 9$ encoded as 32 little-endian bytes (RFC 7748 §6.1).\n\ndef x25519PubkeyL101–103 ↗#Derive the 32-byte X25519 public key from a 32-byte secret key (RFC 7748 §6.1: X25519(secret, 9)).\n\ndef x25519RefL105–107 ↗#Concrete RFC 7748 X25519 Diffie-Hellman function W.C.X : CryptoGames.Ratchet.X25519Fn.\n\ndef x25519PubFromSecretRefL109–111 ↗#Concrete RFC 7748 X25519 public-key derivation W.C.pubKey : CryptoGames.LibsignalSession.PubKeyFn.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/DH/X25519.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.CryptoGames.DH.X25519/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___CryptoGames___DH___X25519"}});